Subject: the floor is not flat: the stereo fold in the space the matrix consumes

D-060, and the correction of D-055's second half.

THE BUG THE USER SAW. On 0.0.0.10, in the headset: "anything that interacts with
the floor feels like it's popping or hovering well above it, when you get close
to it. from afar, things are on the floor fine, don't look weird. but as you
approach, it looks like the objects hover above the floor. dead bodies,
furniture, guns, etc. same with things that intersect with walls. computers or
panels that are on walls, they look like they pop out a bit. doors hurt my eyes
a little when i get close."

THE CAUSE. D-055 left one piece of matrix-shape reasoning in the fold: sm64's
own `P[3][3] > 0.5` gate, which meant "orthographic" and therefore FLAT - no
eye offset, identical in both eyes, ON the panel. Patch 0030's comment named a
dozen sites for it and called §2.4 "simply false" for saying this engine has no
orthographic projection. §2.4 was right. `guOrthoF` is called NOWHERE in this
tree. `camGetOrthogonalMtxL()` is misnamed: player.c:5909 builds it as
mtx4MultMtx4(camGetMtxF1754() /*the perspective*/, &sp8c /*the view*/), which
in this engine's row-vector convention is View * P - the world's own
perspective with the camera baked in. Its [3][3] is the view-space z of the
bg-space origin, so the test fired on it whenever the camera sat on the far
side of the room's own draw offset.

And bgRenderScene() draws THE ENTIRE WORLD through that matrix. The rooms, the
wall hits and the bg translucent pass load camGetOrthogonalMtxL() as their
projection with a per-room TRANSLATION as their modelview (room.c
roomApplyMtx); every prop, chr and door loads camGetPerspectiveMtxL() - the
plain one - with a modelview that already carries the view. So the floor and
the walls were flat on the panel while the body lying on the floor was folded
at its true depth. Far away both are near the panel and nothing looks wrong;
walk up and the floor stays on the glass while the body comes forward. That is
the user's sentence, and it is also his wall panels, his doors and his ramps.

FIVE CHANGES.

  1. The `P[3][3] > 0.5` gate is GONE. The class of a projection is the tag the
     engine put on it (D-055) or the span it is inside, and nothing else.

  2. THE FOLD IS WRITTEN IN THE FORM THAT SURVIVES THE BAKE. The eye matrix
     wanted is View * (T * P') with T the view-space translation and P' the
     convergence skew. Multiplied out against M = View * P, both terms collapse
     onto M itself, because View's last column is (0,0,0,1):

         M'[3][0] = M[3][0] - e*a                  (the translation)
         M'[r][0] = M[r][0] + (a*e/C) * M[r][3]    (the skew, all four rows)

     For a plain P this is ALGEBRAICALLY IDENTICAL to the two lines it
     replaces, which is why 2D parity and the Stereo Depth 0 % identity are
     untouched and the gates still run them. For a baked matrix it is the same
     arithmetic in the only form that can still be written. `a` comes from the
     matrix when the matrix is the plain guPerspectiveF output (an exact test:
     those zeros survive guMtxF2L) and from the last plain load when it is not.

  3. THE BG'S VIEW SPACE IS SCALED. The rooms' view matrix has had its linear
     part multiplied by bgGetScaleBg2Gfx() (player.c, mtx00015f04), so its unit
     is `scale` PD units - a half on Villa, Crash Site and Air Base, and
     anything at all while a scope is zoomed. An eye offset of e VIEW units is
     then e/scale PD units for the floor and e for the body on it: two
     interoculars for one scene. bgRender() now emits the frame's scale as a
     tag and the fold multiplies BOTH e and C by it, so the convergence plane
     stays at the same physical distance and the disparities agree exactly.

  4. THE VIEWMODEL CONVERGES AT ITS NEAREST VERTEX, not its origin. D-055 put
     the origin on the panel, which left the whole muzzle end of the gun in
     FRONT of it, carrying crossed disparity, under an ammo HUD that is 2D at
     zero disparity and drawn over it. The user: "the gun goes behind the ammo
     hud, but the gun, depth wise, is much closer to me, so a little
     disorienting. HUD should probably be closest to me?" It is now: gfx_pc
     measures every gun-classed vertex's clip w and the frame's minimum becomes
     the next frame's convergence plane, frozen across both eye walks. Nothing
     comes in front of the panel, which is quake3e D-028 v2's actual rule, and
     which is also the answer to "the pause menu ... could be closer to me".

  5. LIGHT GLARES GET THE DISPARITY OF THEIR OWN FIXTURE. PD projects a room
     light to the screen itself and draws it as a 2D texture rectangle
     (artifact.c), so it has no matrix, the fold never sees it, and both eyes
     drew it in the same place - a bright, small, high-contrast thing sitting
     on the panel while its fixture is at depth, which is the most
     uncomfortable object a stereo scene can contain. The user: "every time i
     look at a light, it hurts". artifact.c now tags each glare rectangle with
     its view depth and gfx_draw_rectangle shifts it by exactly the disparity
     the world fold would have given a point there, before the aspect
     adjustment, so it lands on its fixture in both eyes.

Counters for all of it in `3d state`: stereo_bg_scale, stereo_p_load,
stereo_p_mul (which settles D-055's unsourced "PD's lists MULTIPLY into the
projection" - read it, do not believe it), stereo_p_baked, stereo_glare_rects,
stereo_gun_near, stereo_gun_verts.

Everywhere but visionOS PD_VISION_STEREO is 0 and every line of this is
preprocessed away; the engine-side tags are under #ifndef PLATFORM_N64 and cost
one G_NOOP each, which changes no draw, tri, vert or texture counter.

